      "notes": [
        "internal/httpapi/branding_handlers_test.go:60 TestBrandingUploadRefusesAnOversizedBodyBeforeReadingItAll is a genuine regression test: I restored main's branding_handlers.go and it failed with 'read 67109009 bytes of a 64MB body', then passed on HEAD. The message assertion alone would have passed pre-fix (the post-parse LimitReader check returns the same 1MB error), so the byte-count assertion is what carries the test - it is doing real work, but it is the only thing separating the two versions.",
        "internal/httpapi/branding_handlers.go:88 The 64KB envelope slack is safe for the only client: web/src/lib/api.ts:324 appends a single 'file' part, so the envelope is boundary + Content-Disposition + Content-Type (a few hundred bytes). A 1MB image still parses (verified by TestBrandingUploadKeepsAcceptingAnImageAtTheLimit), so no previously-accepted upload becomes rejected.",
        "internal/httpapi/branding_handlers.go:90 errors.As against *http.MaxBytesError works here - the oversized test asserts the 1MB message rather than the generic 'read failure' message, which only happens if the As branch is taken. Confirmed on go1.26.7.",
        "Commit message wording is slightly imprecise: ParseMultipartForm(1MB) actually keeps up to maxMemory+10MB in memory before spilling, so uploads under ~11MB were an unbounded-memory issue rather than a disk-spool issue. The disk claim is real above that threshold (the test measured 67MB consumed). Not a defect, just an overstated 'already written to disk'.",
        "web/src/lib/utils.test.ts:49 only exercises the new pure brandingSizeError function; the wiring in web/src/pages/admin-pages.tsx:3457-3465 (clearing the input, skipping the mutation, surfacing pickError) has no component test. The wiring reads correctly - event.target.value = \"\" before upload.mutate(file) is safe since the File reference stays valid after the input is cleared, and clearing first means re-picking the same oversized file fires onChange again.",
        "web/src/pages/admin-pages.tsx:3506 pickError is only recomputed on the next file pick, so an oversized-pick message stays on screen through a subsequent successful URL import or removal. Cosmetic, not a correctness defect.",
        "No auth/permission change: the route stays behind permission(\"settings:write\") at internal/httpapi/server.go:186. No migrations, no external state, no dependency or formatting churn - the diff is entirely on-topic and a single-commit revert restores the prior behaviour exactly.",
        "Verified green: go build ./... and go test ./... pass, web tsc --noEmit exits 0, vitest 18/18 pass."
      ],
